WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.190
The term "cult classic" gets tossed around
a little too much, but is it really always

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:05.680
justified?

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:10.380
On occasion, a movie is so poorly made that
it finds its way into the "cult classic" conversation

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.040
by way of the "so bad it's great" loophole.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:17.740
More often than not, "so bad" just means they're
actually terrible films, kinda like the ones

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:18.700
on this list.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:20.540
Children Of The Corn

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:25.220
Adapted from Stephen King’s short story,
Children Of The Corn takes place in an isolated

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:29.730
Nebraska town and follows a child-led religious
cult, hell bent on ensuring that the town's

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:31.340
corn crop remains robust.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.680
Of course, they have to sacrifice all of the
adults to make that happen.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:38.770
That last bit bodes poorly for an out-of-town
couple driving through their fields.

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:41.020
“We must sacrifice them both tonight.”

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.160
“Amos will satisfy him.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.560
We need the woman, she’ll bring the man
to us.”

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:46.240
“No...”

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.380
So what went wrong with Children of the Corn?

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:53.410
King tried to adapt it for the big screen
himself, but the author's moody, character-driven

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:58.730
screenplay was tossed aside in favor of George
Goldsmith's ludicrous, hyper-violent, narratively

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:00.120
flimsy take on the tale.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:04.180
Even if some of the film's imagery still colors
the pop-culture landscape, Children of the

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:09.060
Corn is a stagnant and ugly film to look at,
leading us to question just how this film

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.940
managed to inspire its own straight-to-video
franchise.

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:13.580
Grandma's Boy

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.780
There's no accounting for taste when you're
talking about cult classics.

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:21.049
Still, every once in a while, a film claims
cult status that just makes you want to hold

00:01:21.049 --> 00:01:22.049
people accountable.

00:01:22.049 --> 00:01:25.360
Case in point: 2006's stoner comedy Grandma's
Boy.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:29.460
Understand that we're using the word "comedy"
loosely here, because there's really not much

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:33.680
to laugh at in this tale of a 35-year-old
slacker forced to move in with his Grandma.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.120
Realistically, there are only about four actual
jokes in Grandma's Boy, and the film simply

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.920
recycles them over and over and over again.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:41.880
“What’s in here?”

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:46.860
“That’s just the kids in the their break
room, or as I call it the stupid idiot room.”

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:52.170
We get it, they smoke a lot of pot, play a
lot of video games, and that one guy acts

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:52.840
like a robot.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:53.740
What else you got?

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.700
The answer is a resounding "Nothing."

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:57.240
The Room

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:02.780
In 2003 Tommy Wiseau unleashed The Room on
the world, and almost in spite of itself,

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:05.389
it's become a bonafide cult sensation over
the years.

00:02:05.389 --> 00:02:09.840
James Franco even took on the feature-length
re-enactment of what went on behind the scenes

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.000
with The Disaster Artist.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.040
We can't help but be a little worried that
people are taking Wiseau's disasterpiece seriously.

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:19.480
Wiseau's tale of a good man losing it all
looks and feels like a no-budget TV show from

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:24.080
the early 90s, the dialogue is often unintelligible,
and the acting is embarrassingly over the

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:24.580
top.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:25.620
“I did not hit her.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:26.620
It’s not true.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:27.340
It’s bulls---.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:28.340
I did not hit her.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:29.540
I did not.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:31.260
Oh, hi Mark.”

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:35.980
To make matters worse, it doesn't look like
any of the cast, save for Wiseau, has a clue

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.000
what's going to happen from one moment to
the next.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:41.980
We get it, everyone loves to watch a disaster,
but that doesn't mean the disaster deserves

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:43.040
to be celebrated.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:44.480
Showgirls

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:50.140
So you've got a friend who read a book that
says Paul Verhoeven's 1995 sexploitation satire

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:52.140
Showgirls is a misunderstood masterpiece?

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.660
“Nomi, what kind of turn was that?

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:55.860
Do a pique turn.

00:02:58.100 --> 00:02:58.660
That’s it.”

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:04.090
With all due respect to your “friend,”
here are three irrefutable facts that prove

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:05.930
just how bad Showgirls is.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:09.219
One: Kyle Maclachlan is still shocked by just
how bad it turned out.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:12.650
In a 2012 interview he admitted to being "gobsmacked"
after seeing it.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:17.300
Two: Elizabeth Berkley, a trained dancer in
real life, gave up dancing for years after

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:18.100
the film's release.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:23.260
Three: Showgirls basically ended the career
of Berkley, as well as screenwriter Joe Eszterhas,

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:26.220
who was one of Hollywood's hottest writers
at the time.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:30.600
Although one could argue Verhoeven was brilliant
to exploit the script's ridiculous plot and

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:35.700
misguided sense of feminism, its record-setting
run at the 1995 Razzie Awards proves our point.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:36.600
Dune

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:41.659
There's not a filmmaker on this list with
more cult-cred than David Lynch, but even

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:46.240
the famed auteur couldn't "cult" his way into
bringing a successful version of Frank Herbert's

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:48.160
sci-fi novel Dune to the big screen.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:03.620
Lynch's big-budget debut hit theaters in 1984
and was immediately marked as an epic misfire

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:04.120
by critics.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:07.080
In his one-star review of the film, Roger
Ebert stated,

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.840
"This movie is a real mess....One of the most
confusing screenplays of all time."

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:14.020
If you've sat through all two hours and 20
minutes of the film yourself, you know it's

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:14.520
true.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:18.140
Though Lynch shoulders much of the blame,
he's not entirely at fault.

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:21.240
Dune was, after all, just his third feature
film as director.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:25.960
After small-scale successes with Eraserhead
and The Elephant Man, Dune was a dramatic

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:28.530
shift in both scale and story for Lynch.

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:29.600
Maybe a little too big.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:33.610
Matters only got worse in post-production
when financiers wrestled control of Dune away

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:34.300
from Lynch.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:36.320
The result was an absolute mess of a film.

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:40.759
The sort of mess that makes us wonder whether
its "cult classic" status isn't merely a reflection

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:41.920
on the man who directed it.

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:43.440
Mommie Dearest

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:49.440
Even if you haven't seen bizarro 1981 melodrama
Mommie Dearest, you can almost certainly quote

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:50.540
its most famous line.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:56.540
“No wire hangers!”

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:01.830
Please trust us when we advise you to leave
your exposure to Mommie Dearest to that singular

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:05.680
moment because the rest of the film only gets
weirder, and not in a good way.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:10.280
Part soap opera drama and part psychological
horror film, Mommie Dearest takes its story

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:13.520
from a memoir penned by Joan Crawford's adopted
daughter Christina.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:17.520
At the center of it all is a performance from
Faye Dunaway that can only be described as

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.080
really, really big; so big it ends up dwarfing
the work of everyone she shared the screen

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:22.580
with.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:26.400
It's the sort of performance that most directors
would beg an actor to tone down.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:30.900
Unfortunately, Mommie Dearest director Frank
Perry let Dunaway run with it, and she ran

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:34.100
his film right into the realm of caustic,
unwatchable camp.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:35.480
Howard The Duck

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:40.880
Howard The Duck was destined to claim "cult
classic" status before cameras even rolled,

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.120
mostly because the Marvel comic that spawned
it was the very definition of cult fiction.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:49.180
At the center of that comic was a wise-cracking,
cigar-chomping, kung-fu-fighting fowl who

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:50.480
goes by the name of Howard.

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:51.580
“Fly away!”

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:52.140
“Shoo!”

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:54.820
“No one laughs at a Master of quack-foo.”

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:02.000
Yes, Howard The Duck is every bit as bonkers
as it sounds.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.699
It was produced as a live-action feature even
though director William Huyck believed it

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:08.370
would work better as animation.

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:12.320
The film's producer George Lucas was the one
who insisted that the project go ahead with

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:16.449
bold, practical effects and, um, a dwarf in
a duck suit.

00:06:16.449 --> 00:06:19.840
We're pretty sure Lucas regretted that decision
once Howard The Duck was finished.

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:24.250
The seemingly ready-made "cult classic" proved
an absolute eye-sore of a film.

